From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 01:50:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] soc: apple: sleep: give each step of the way back its
 own WD2 window

A T8132 deep sleep that commits and then hangs on the way back ends in
a WD2 reset that takes the kernel log, pstore included, with it. The
next boot sees only the PMU's failed-boot stage (0xe3), which iBoot's
sleep path writes either way:

  macsmc-reboot macsmc-reboot: previous boot: failed-boot stage 0xe3

The resume stub restarts WD2 for 60 s and apple_sleep_return() later
sets WDT_SUSPEND_SECS (120 s). A J604 whose sleep reset about 60 s
after its RTC wake therefore stopped somewhere between the two, which
is most of the way back.

Split that span: 80 s from the first line of apple_sleep_return() and
100 s once the clusters, gates and domains are back. The time from the
wake to the next boot then names the step that stopped, without
touching SPMI, which macOS leaves alone for a long time after a wake.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
 drivers/soc/apple/sleep.c | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/soc/apple/sleep.c b/drivers/soc/apple/sleep.c
index 928343259e..baf93f9e75 100644
--- a/drivers/soc/apple/sleep.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/apple/sleep.c
@@ -148,6 +148,13 @@ struct aura_s2r_mailbox {
 #define WDT_SLEEP_SECS		5
 /* Device suspend and resume can take a while, and nothing else recovers a hang in them. */
 #define WDT_SUSPEND_SECS	120
+/*
+ * The way back has its own WD2 windows, each a different length, so the time from the
+ * wake to a reset says which step stopped: the resume stub's 60 s, then these, then
+ * WDT_SUSPEND_SECS over device resume. Nothing else survives that reset.
+ */
+#define WDT_RETURN_SECS		80
+#define WDT_REWAKE_SECS		100
 
 #define AON_AP_DEADLINE		0x08
 
@@ -3250,6 +3257,9 @@ static void apple_sleep_return(struct apple_sleep *s)
 	u32 val;
 	int i;
 
+	/* Back in C with the MMU on. */
+	apple_sleep_set_wd2(s, WDT_RETURN_SECS);
+
 	/*
 	 * The reset leaves the timer control at its EL2 default, without the event stream
 	 * or EL0's counter. The timer driver's CPU PM exit puts it back too, later.
@@ -3293,6 +3303,9 @@ static void apple_sleep_return(struct apple_sleep *s)
 	writel(PWRGATE_ON, s->pwrgate + s->soc->pwrgate_cpm[0]);
 	writel(PWRGATE_ON, s->pwrgate + s->soc->pwrgate_cpm[1]);
 
+	/* The clusters, the gates and the domains are back. */
+	apple_sleep_set_wd2(s, WDT_REWAKE_SECS);
+
 	sleep_dvfm_unpark(s);
 
 	/* macOS stops it here. Keep a longer one over device resume, until .end. */
-- 
2.43.0

